<description>
Reorient each two-pixel blue/pink segment so it reads pink then blue when viewed from its border marker.

Input structure
- The background is dominant, with one border-only marker color and blue/pink foreground segments.
- Every marker lies on the outer border and points inward along its row or column.
- Along exactly one inward ray from each marker is a single adjacent two-pixel blue/pink segment.

Rule
- For each marker, look inward perpendicular to the border side containing the marker.
- Rewrite the two blue/pink pixels on that ray so the nearer one is pink and the farther one is blue.
- Keep marker pixels, background pixels, and all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The marker gives a local viewing direction; the segment's required order is relative to that marker, not to global row or column order.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "border marker pixel s",
  "dst": "adjacent blue/pink segment d on an inward row or column ray",
  "match_cond": "d lies on the unique inward ray from s",
  "transform": "rewrite d so the pixel nearer s is pink and the farther pixel is blue"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 8-18 rows x 8-18 cols, always square [observed]
  colors: background=azure(8), marker=orange(7), segment_pair=blue(1)+pink(6)
  markers:
    count: 4-14 orange pixels, all on the outer border (row 0, last row, col 0, last col)
    corner_allowed: yes, but exactly one inward ray must contain a segment
    distribution: spread across 2-4 border sides; multiple markers may share a row or column
  segments:
    count: equals marker count; each is an adjacent 2-cell pair of one blue + one pink pixel
    orientation: aligned with its marker ray (horizontal segment for left/right marker, vertical for top/bottom)
    placement: on the unique inward ray from exactly one marker, at distance 2-14 cells from marker
    no_overlap: no ray hits >2 colored pixels; no pixel belongs to two segments; no segment shared by two markers
    initial_order: ~30-70% already pink-then-blue (correct); remainder blue-then-pink (need swap)
  rule: for each marker, rewrite its segment so the nearer pixel is pink(6) and the farther is blue(1)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size N x N, N in [8..18] even preferred [observed]
    2. Choose marker count K in [4..14]
    3. Place K marker pixels on distinct border cells (avoid placing two that would create conflicting rays to same interior cell)
    4. For each marker, determine its unique inward direction (corner markers have two candidates -- pick one)
    5. Build the OUTPUT state -- for each marker, place a correctly-oriented segment (pink nearer, blue farther) at a random distance 2..N-2 along its ray, ensuring no ray collision with other segments or markers
    6. Derive the INPUT by randomly flipping ~30-70% of segments from [pink,blue] to [blue,pink]; keep the rest already correct
    7. Validate: every marker sees exactly one 2-cell adjacent segment on exactly one inward ray; blue count == pink count == K
